<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Free and Independent State of Cundinamarca</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 CE – 1815 CE · capital Santa Fe de Bogotá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A centralist republic around Bogotá that fought rival federalists during Colombia's 'Foolish Fatherland' before the Spanish reconquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> revolution of 20 July 1810, the province of Santa Fe proclaimed the State of Cundinamarca. Its constitution of 1811, under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-tadeo-lozano">Jorge Tadeo Lozano</a>, still recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-vii-of-spain">Ferdinand VII</a> as king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-narino">Antonio Nariño</a>, who had translated the Rights of Man in 1794, soon took power and favoured a strong central government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cundinamarca refused to join the federalist <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-provinces-of-new-granada">United Provinces of New Granada</a>, and the two sides fought a civil war in 1812 and 1813. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-narino">Nariño</a> repelled a federalist attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> in January 1813, and in July Cundinamarca declared full independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Nariño then led a campaign against royalists in the south but was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasto">Pasto</a> in 1814.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In December 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">United Provinces</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> and forced Cundinamarca to join the federation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-morillo">Pablo Morillo's</a> royalist army retook Bogotá in 1816, ending the first period of independence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca.jpg" alt="Free and Independent State of Cundinamarca" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cundinamarca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1810 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Fe de Bogotá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jorge Tadeo Lozano, Antonio Nariño, Manuel de Bernardo Álvarez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>State of Cundinamarca, Cundinamarca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca" aria-label="Free and Independent State of Cundinamarca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2626 2006 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free and Independent State of Cundinamarca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2946 2262L2946 2260M2946 2262L2967 2226"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2946 2260h0M2967 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2946 2262h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2946 2262h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
